#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *
#import "diagrams/18-cartan-brauer.typ": *

== The Cartan-Brauer Triangle <sec:cartan-brauer-triangle>

In §@sec:decomposition-map, we defined the decomposition map
$d:G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ for an arbitrary $p$-modular system
$(K,R,k)$. In this section we assume that $R G$ is a semiperfect ring (see
§@ss:projective-covers). Let $K_0 lr((R G))$ denote the Grothendieck group
associated with the category $cal(P)(R G)$ of f.g. projective $R G$-modules, and
let $K_0 lr((k G))$ be the Grothendieck group of the category $cal(P)(k G)$. We
prove first that there is a natural isomorphism
$ K_0 lr((R G)) tilde.equiv K_0 lr((k G)) $
obtained by reduction mod $frak(p)$. We then prove that there exists a
commutative diagram called the _Cartan-Brauer Triangle_,
#idx("Cartan-Brauer triangle")
#cartan-brauer-triangle
#source(450)
where $d$ is the decomposition map and $c$ the Cartan homomorphism (see
@def:cartan-homomorphism-matrix). In §@ss:cartan-brauer-large-field, the
properties of the Cartan-Brauer Triangle are derived when $K$ is sufficiently
large (§@ss:modular-splitting-fields), and are then applied to derive
orthogonality relations for the Brauer characters of the simple $k G$-modules
and their projective covers. The point of view taken in this section is due to
Swan (@bib:Swan1960, @bib:Swan1963; see also Serre @bib:Serre1977). Extensions
of the results in various directions will appear in
§§@sec:induction-arbitrary-fields, @sec:projective-lattices, and Chapters~10
and~11 of Volume~II below.

#heading(level: 3)[
  The Cartan Map and the Cartan-Brauer Triangle
] <ss:cartan-map-triangle>

Throughout this section, $(K,R,k)$ denotes a $p$-modular system such that $R G$
is a semiperfect ring (see §@ss:projective-covers). We do not assume, in this
subsection, that $K$ is sufficiently large or of characteristic zero.

The hypothesis that $R G$ be semiperfect holds whenever $R$ is complete in the
$frak(p)$-adic topology, by §@ss:krull-schmidt-azumaya. In particular, this is
the case whenever $K$ is the completion of some algebraic number field with
respect to a discrete valuation, and $R$ is the valuation ring in $K$. The
condition on $R G$ also holds for a d.v.r. $R$ in an arbitrary field $K$,
provided that $K$ is a sufficiently large field such that
$char K divides.not |G|$ (see Exercise~@exc:split-order-idempotent-lifting).

For the convenience of the reader, we begin with a brief summary of results from
§§@sec:radicals, @sec:idempotents-krull-schmidt-projective-covers concerning the
structure of $R G$, $k G$ and their modules.

Let $N=rad R G$, $frak(p)G=frak(p) dot (R G)$, and recall from
@prop:finite-algebra-local-base-radical that $frak(p)G subset.eq N$ and
$R G slash N tilde.equiv k G slash rad k G$. The latter isomorphism implies that
simple $k G$-modules can be identified with simple $R G$-modules.

As in §@sec:decomposition-map, we shall denote $R G slash frak(p)G$ by
$overline(R G)$ or by $k G$; we let $a -> overline(a)$ denote the natural map
from $R$ to $k$, or from $R G$ to $k G$. For a left $R G$-module $M$, we let
$overline(M)$ stand for the $k G$-module $M slash frak(p)M$. The decomposition
map $d:G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ (see @def:decomposition-map-matrix)
assigns to each element $[V]$ in $G_0 lr((K G))$ corresponding to a $K G$-module
$V$, the element $[overline(M)]$ in $G_0 lr((k G))$, where $M$ is any full
$R G$-lattice in $V$.

The condition that $R G$ is semiperfect means that $R G slash N$ is artinian,
and that every idempotent in $R G slash N$ is the image of an idempotent in
$R G$. By §@ss:krull-schmidt-azumaya, the ring $k G$ is always semiperfect. From
the theory of projective covers in §@ss:projective-covers, f.g. projective
modules over $R G$ and $k G$, respectively, decompose into indecomposable
summands, which are unique up to isomorphism and order of occurrence.

#statement("Summary", "prop", italic: true)[
  Let $(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular system such that $R G$ is a semiperfect ring.
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [Let ${F_1,dots,F_r}$ be a basic set of simple $k G$-modules. Each module
      $F_i$ has an indecomposable projective cover $U_i$, which is isomorphic to
      a left ideal in $k G$ generated by a primitive idempotent. Each
      indecomposable projective module $U_i$ has a unique maximal submodule
      $rad U_i$, and $U_i slash rad U_i tilde.equiv F_i$ for each $i$. Note that
      $rad U_i=(rad k G)U_i$ by @prop:semilocal-module-radical.],
    [
      #source(451)
      Each f.g. projective $k G$-module $U$ can be expressed as an external
      direct sum
      $ U tilde.equiv product.co_i U_i^((n_i)), $
      with uniquely determined multiplicities ${n_i}$.
    ],
    [By means of the isomorphism $R G slash N tilde.equiv k G slash rad k G$,
      the simple modules ${F_1,dots,F_r}$ can be identified with a basic set of
      simple $R G$-modules. Each simple $R G$-module $F_i$ has an indecomposable
      projective cover $P_i in cal(P)(R G)$ such that
      $overline(P_i) tilde.equiv U_i$, $1<=i<=r$. The module $P_i$ can be taken
      to be a left ideal $R G e_i$, where $e_i$ is a primitive idempotent in
      $R G$ such that $overline(e_i)$ is primitive in $k G$, and
      $k G overline(e_i) tilde.equiv U_i$.],
    [Every f.g. projective $R G$-module $M$ can be expressed as an external
      direct sum
      $ M tilde.equiv product.co_i P_i^((m_i)), $
      with uniquely determined multiplicities ${m_i}$. Two modules $M$ and $M'$
      in $cal(P)(R G)$ are isomorphic if and only if
      $overline(M) tilde.equiv overline(M')$. A module $M in cal(P)(R G)$ is
      indecomposable if and only if $overline(M)$ is indecomposable.],
    [Each module $M in cal(P)(R G)$ is the projective cover of the semisimple
      module $M slash rad M$. The module $M in cal(P)(R G)$ is indecomposable if
      and only if $M slash rad M$ is simple, and in that case, $rad M$ is the
      unique maximal submodule of $M$.],
    [Each $U_i$ has a unique minimal submodule $op("soc") U_i$ (its socle), and
      $ op("soc") U_i tilde.equiv F_i,1<=i<=r. $
      Further, $U_i$ is the injective hull of $F_i$.],
  )
] <prop:projective-cover-summary>

References for the proofs of the results summarized in
@prop:projective-cover-summary are as follows. Radicals of modules are discussed
in §@ss:radicals-basic-definitions. Statement (i) follows from
@th:complete-ring-idempotent-decompositions and
@cor:indecomposable-projective-top. A module $U in cal(P)(k G)$ is f.g., hence a
direct sum of indecomposable modules. The summands are projective because $U$ is
projective. The uniqueness statement in (ii) follows from the K-S-A Theorem.

The existence and uniqueness of projective covers of simple $R G$-modules is
proved in @th:semiperfect-projective-covers. The fact that
$overline(P_i) tilde.equiv U_i$ follows because $overline(P_i)$ is projective
(as a direct summand of $overline(R G)$), and both $overline(P_i)$ and $U_i$ are
projective covers of $F_i$ for $1<=i<=r$. The last statement in (iii) is left as
an exercise for the reader.

The first part of (iv) follows by the same argument used to prove (ii). The last
part of (iv) and all of (v) are immediate from the theory of projective covers
(see @prop:projective-isomorphism-classes-quotient,
@th:semiperfect-projective-covers and
@cor:semiperfect-projective-cover-properties). Finally, (vi) follows from
@prop:symmetric-projective-top-socle.

#source(452)
We recall from §@sec:decomposition-map the notation $K_0 lr((k G))$ for the
Grothendieck group of the category $cal(P)(k G)$ of f.g. projective
$k G$-modules, and $K_0 lr((R G))$ for the Grothendieck group of $cal(P)(R G)$.
Note that each ses $0 -> P' -> P -> P'' -> 0$ of modules from $cal(P)(R G)$ must
split, because $P''$ is projective. Thus the defining relations of
$K_0 lr((R G))$ can be expressed in the simpler form
$ [P' plus.o P'']=[P']+[P''],P',P'' in cal(P)(R G). $
Similar remarks apply to $cal(P)(k G)$.

By @prop:semiperfect-projective-class-basis, $K_0 lr((R G))$ and $K_0 lr((k G))$
are free abelian groups with bases ${[P_1],dots,[P_r]}$ and ${[U_1],dots,[U_r]}$
corresponding to basic sets of indecomposable projective modules in
$cal(P)(R G)$ and $cal(P)(k G)$, respectively. By
@prop:projective-cover-summary(iii), these bases can be chosen so that
$[overline(P_i)]=[U_i]$ for $1<=i<=r$. We now have

#theorem[
  The map $[M] |-> [overline(M)]$, for $M in cal(P)(R G)$, defines an
  isomorphism of abelian groups:
  $ K_0 lr((R G)) tilde.equiv K_0 lr((k G)). $
] <th:projective-class-reduction-isomorphism>
#proof[
  By the preceding remarks, there is an isomorphism of abelian groups
  $K_0 lr((R G)) tilde.equiv K_0 lr((k G))$, given by
  $ sum_(i=1)^r a_i[P_i] -> sum_(i=1)^r a_i[overline(P_i)],a_i in ZZ. $
  We need only check that this map carries $[M]$ onto $[overline(M)]$ for all
  $M in cal(P)(R G)$. By @prop:projective-cover-summary(iv), we have
  $ M tilde.equiv product.co_(i=1)^r P_i^((m_i)) $
  for some non-negative integers ${m_i}$. Since the relations in $K_0 lr((R G))$
  are given by direct sum decompositions, we obtain
  $ [M]=sum_(i=1)^r m_i[P_i] quad "in" K_0 lr((R G)). $
  On the other hand, the decomposition of $M$ yields
  $ overline(M) tilde.equiv product.co_(i=1)^r overline(P_i)^((m_i)), $
  and hence
  $ [overline(M)]=sum_(i=1)^r m_i[overline(P_i)] quad "in" K_0 lr((k G)). $
  Thus $[M]$ goes to $[overline(M)]$ under the isomorphism, and the proof is
  complete.
]

#source(453)
It is easily checked that the map
$ P -> K times.o_R P,P in cal(P)(R G), $
defines a homomorphism of abelian groups from $K_0 lr((R G))$ to
$G_0 lr((K G))$. Composing this map with the isomorphism
$K_0 lr((k G)) -> K_0 lr((R G))$ given in
@th:projective-class-reduction-isomorphism, we obtain a homomorphism of abelian
groups:
$ e:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((K G)). $ <eq:projective-character-map>

Next, let $U in cal(P)(k G)$, and let $c_i lr((U))$ be the uniquely determined
multiplicity of the simple module $F_i$ as a composition factor of $U$. By
@prop:grothendieck-group-universal-property and
@prop:artinian-grothendieck-simple-basis, there exists an additive homomorphism
$ c:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((k G)), $
given by
$ c[U]=[U]=sum_(i=1)^r c_i lr((U))[F_i] quad "for each" U "in" cal(P)(k G). $

#definition[
  The homomorphism
  $ c:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((k G)) $
  defined above is called the _Cartan homomorphism_. The $r times r$ _Cartan
  matrix_ $bold(C)=(c_(i j))$ of $k G$ is defined by
  $ c[U_i]=sum_(j=1)^r c_(i j)[F_j],1<=i<=r. $
  Thus the Cartan matrix is the transpose of the matrix of $c$ with respect to
  the ordered bases ${[U_i]}$ and ${[F_j]}$ of $K_0 lr((k G))$ and
  $G_0 lr((k G))$, respectively.
] <def:cartan-homomorphism-matrix>
#idx("Cartan homomorphism", target: [@def:cartan-homomorphism-matrix])
#idx("Cartan matrix", target: [@def:cartan-homomorphism-matrix])

#proposition[
  The _Cartan-Brauer triangle_
  #cartan-brauer-triangle
  where $d$ is the decomposition map, $c$ is the Cartan homomorphism, and $e$ is
  defined by @eq:projective-character-map, is a commutative diagram of additive
  groups and additive homomorphisms.
] <prop:cartan-brauer-commutative-triangle>
#idx(
  "Cartan-Brauer triangle",
  target: [@prop:cartan-brauer-commutative-triangle],
)
#proof[
  #source(454)
  We have to prove that $d(e[U])=c[U]$ for each $U in cal(P)(k G)$. From
  @prop:projective-cover-summary, it follows that there exists
  $P in cal(P)(R G)$ such that $overline(P)=U$. Then by the definition of $e$,
  $e[U]=[K times.o_R P]$. Evidently $P$ is a full $R G$-lattice in
  $K times.o_R P$, so by @prop:decomposition-homomorphism-existence we have
  $ d(e[U])=[overline(P)]=[U] quad "in" G_0 lr((k G)). $
  On the other hand, the element $[U] in G_0 lr((k G))$ is the image $c[U]$ of
  the element $[U] in K_0 lr((k G))$, and hence $d(e[U])=c[U]$, as required.
]

By @cor:tensor-projective-group-lattice, it follows that $K_0 lr((k G))$ is a
ring#footnote[$K_0 lr((k G))$ need not have an identity element, however.]. It
is then clear that the maps ${c,d,e}$ in
@prop:cartan-brauer-commutative-triangle are homomorphisms of rings.

#heading(level: 3)[
  Properties of the Cartan-Brauer Triangle ($K$ Sufficiently Large)
] <ss:cartan-brauer-large-field>

In this subsection, we continue to require that $(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular
system such that $R G$ is a semiperfect ring. We shall assume furthermore that
$K$ is sufficiently large, and that $char K=0$. As pointed out in
§@ss:cartan-map-triangle, these assumptions on $K$ guarantee that $R G$ is
semiperfect for each d.v.r. $R$. Further, by §@ss:modular-splitting-fields we
know that both $K$ and $k$ are splitting fields for $G$ and all of its
subgroups.

The properties of the Cartan-Brauer Triangle in this situation are fundamental
for deeper investigations of Brauer characters, and are equivalent, for the most
part, to properties of the Cartan matrix $bold(C)$ and the decomposition matrix
$bold(D)$. Most of the results are due to Brauer-Nesbitt @bib:Brauer1941, whose
approach was presented in @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR §§82–84]. As we noted in the
introduction to §@sec:cartan-brauer-triangle, our treatment here follows Swan
(@bib:Swan1960, @bib:Swan1963) and Serre @bib:Serre1977.

As in §@sec:orthogonality-central-idempotents, let ${Z_1,dots,Z_s}$ denote the
simple $K G$-modules, and ${zeta^1,dots,zeta^s}$ the $K$-characters afforded by
them. We let
$ (zeta,zeta')=|G|^(-1)sum_(x in G) zeta(x)zeta'(x^(-1)), $
<eq:character-pairing-cartan-brauer>
for $K$-characters $zeta$ and $zeta'$. Since $K$ is a splitting field, the
irreducible characters ${zeta^1,zeta^2,dots,zeta^s}$ form an orthonormal basis
of $cf_K lr((G))$ with respect to the bilinear form $(zeta,zeta')$, by
Proposition~@prop:group-character-orthogonality-idempotents and the discussion
in §@ss:finite-group-character-orthogonality. Moreover, by
@prop:character-scalar-products-multiplicities we have
$ (zeta,zeta')=i(M,M'), $ <eq:character-pairing-intertwining>
for $K G$-modules $M$ and $M'$ affording the characters $zeta$ and $zeta'$,
respectively. Here $i(M,M')$ is the intertwining number defined by
$ i(M,M')=dim_K Hom_(K G)(M,M'). $

#source(455)
Since $G_0 lr((K G)) tilde.equiv "ch" K G$ by @prop:grothendieck-character-ring,
we may define a $ZZ$-bilinear form
$ i_K:G_0 lr((K G)) times G_0 lr((K G)) -> ZZ $
corresponding to the bilinear form on $"ch" K G$ defined by
@eq:character-pairing-cartan-brauer. Using @eq:character-pairing-intertwining,
the form $i_K$ is defined by
$ i_K lr(([M],[M']))=i(M,M')=(zeta,zeta') $
for f.g. $K G$-modules $M$ and $M'$ affording the $K$-characters $zeta$ and
$zeta'$, respectively. By the orthogonality relations (see
@prop:character-scalar-products-multiplicities), we have
$ i_K lr(([Z_i],[Z_j]))=delta_(i j),1<=i,j<=s. $

Turning to the residue field $k$, we have:

#proposition[
  Let $k$ be a splitting field for $G$. There exists a bilinear map
  $ i_k:K_0 lr((k G)) times G_0 lr((k G)) -> ZZ $
  such that #named-equation(name: [$(ast)$])[
    $ i_k lr(([U],[M]))=dim_k lr((Hom_(k G)(U,M))) $
  ] <eq:projective-simple-pairing-definition>
  for all modules $U in cal(P)(k G)$ and
  $M in attach(op("mod"), bl: k G)$.#footnote[
    $attach(op("mod"), bl: k G)$ denotes the category of f.g. left
    $k G$-modules.
  ] Moreover, letting ${U_1,dots,U_r}$ and ${F_1,dots,F_r}$ denote corresponding
  basic sets of indecomposable projective and simple $k G$-modules,
  respectively, we have
  $ i_k lr(([U_i],[F_j]))=delta_(i j),1<=i,j<=r. $
] <prop:projective-simple-grothendieck-pairing>
#proof[
  We first define $i_k$ by @eq:projective-simple-pairing-definition above, and
  then extend by linearity to a map
  $ i_k:K_0 lr((k G)) times G_0 lr((k G)) -> ZZ. $
  We have to show that $i_k$ is well defined. First, let
  $ 0 -> M' -> M -> M'' -> 0 $
  be a ses of $k G$-modules, and let $U in cal(P)(k G)$. Then the sequence
  $ 0 -> Hom_(k G)(U,M') -> Hom_(k G)(U,M) -> Hom_(k G)(U,M'') -> 0 $
  is exact. This follows from @prop:projective-module-criteria, since the
  functor $Hom(U, dot)$ is exact whenever $U$ is projective. On the other hand,
  if
  $ 0 -> U' -> U -> U'' -> 0 $
  #source(456)
  is a ses in $cal(P)(k G)$, then $U tilde.equiv U' plus.o U''$, and hence
  $ Hom_(k G)(U,M) tilde.equiv Hom_(k G)(U',M) plus.o Hom_(k G)(U'',M) $
  for all f.g. $k G$-modules $M$. From these remarks, it follows that $i_k$ is a
  well defined bilinear map on the Grothendieck groups.

  To prove the second statement, let us assume that the ${U_i}$ and ${F_i}$
  correspond to each other as in §@ss:cartan-map-triangle. Then by
  @prop:projective-cover-summary, $rad U_i$ is the unique maximal submodule of
  $U_i$, and $U_i slash rad U_i tilde.equiv F_i$, $1<=i<=r$. Since $k$ is a
  splitting field, it follows that
  $ dim_k lr((Hom_(k G)(U_i,F_j)))=delta_(i j),1<=i,j<=r, $
  completing the proof.
]

#theorem[
  The decomposition map
  $ d:G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((k G)), $
  and the map
  $ e:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((K G)) $
  (defined by @eq:projective-character-map), are transposes of one another with
  respect to the bilinear forms $i_k$ and $i_K$. More precisely, we have
  $
    i_k lr((u,d z))=i_K lr((e u,z)) quad forall u in K_0 lr((k G)),
    z in G_0 lr((K G)).
  $
] <th:decomposition-projective-character-adjointness>
#proof[
  It is sufficient to prove the result for generators of $K_0 lr((k G))$ and
  $G_0 lr((K G))$, so we let $u=[U]$ and $z=[Z]$, for modules $U in cal(P)(k G)$
  and $Z in attach(op("mod"), bl: K G)$. Since $R G$ is semiperfect, we may
  apply Theorem~@th:projective-class-reduction-isomorphism to obtain an element
  $P in cal(P)(R G)$ such that $overline(P)=U$; then $e[U]=K times.o_R P$. On
  the other hand, let $L$ be a full $R G$-lattice in $Z$; then
  $d[Z]=[overline(L)]$ in $G_0 lr((k G))$. By the Change of Rings
  Theorem~@th:change-of-rings,
  $
    K times.o_R Hom_(R G)(P,L) tilde.equiv
    Hom_(K G)(K times.o_R P,K times.o_R L).
  $
  But also $Hom_(R G)(R G,L) tilde.equiv L$ as $R G$-modules, and therefore
  $
    overline(Hom_(R G)(R G,L)) tilde.equiv overline(L)
    tilde.equiv Hom_(k G)(k G,overline(L)).
  $
  Since $P$ is projective and $Hom$ is additive, the above result implies that
  $
    overline(Hom_(R G)(P,L)) tilde.equiv
    Hom_(k G)(overline(P),overline(L)).
  $
  #source(457)
  Combining these observations, we obtain
  $
    i_K lr(
      (
        e[U],[Z]
      )
    ) & =dim_K lr((Hom_(K G)(K times.o_R P,K times.o_R L))) \
      & =dim_k lr((Hom_(k G)(overline(P),overline(L))))
        =dim_k lr((Hom_(k G)(U,overline(L))))
        =i_k lr(([U],d[Z])),
  $
  as required.
]

#corollary[
  Let $bold(C)$ be the Cartan matrix and $bold(D)$ the decomposition matrix of
  $G$, for the $p$-modular system $(K,R,k)$. Then $bold(C)$ is a symmetric
  matrix, and we have
  $ bold(C)=attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(D). $
] <cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix>
#proof[
  Let $attach(bold(E), tl: t)$ be the matrix of the map
  $e:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((K G))$, with respect to the bases
  ${[U_i]}_(1<=i<=r)$ and ${[Z_j]}_(1<=j<=s)$. By the preceding theorem, we have
  $bold(E)=attach(bold(D), tl: t)$. On the other hand, $c=d compose e$, by
  Proposition~@prop:cartan-brauer-commutative-triangle. Using the definition of
  the matrices $bold(C)$ and $bold(D)$, we obtain
  $ bold(C)=bold(E) dot bold(D)=attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(D). $
  Thus $attach(bold(C), tl: t)=bold(C)$, and the proof is complete.
]

For another version of this proof, in different notation, see
@bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR (83.9)].

#corollary[
  Assume that $p divides.not |G|$. Then $G=G_(p')$, $r=s$, $bold(C)$ is the
  identity matrix and $bold(D)$ is a permutation matrix. Every Brauer character
  of a $k G$-module coincides with the $K$-character of a $K G$-module.
] <cor:prime-to-p-brauer-ordinary-characters>

The proof is immediate from Corollary~@cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix,
since in this case $k G$ is a split semisimple $k$-algebra, and the number of
simple $k G$-modules equals the number of conjugacy classes of $G$, by
@th:split-group-algebra-dimensions. For a direct proof of this corollary, see
Exercise~@exc:prime-to-p-projective-lifts.

The next result is the source of several other properties of the Cartan-Brauer
triangle, and is of independent interest.

#theorem(title: [The Brauer lift])[
  Let $lambda$ be the Brauer character of a left $k G$-module $L$, and extend
  $lambda$ to a class function $hat(lambda)$ on $G$ by setting
  $ hat(lambda)(x)=lambda(s),x in G, $
  where $s$ is the $p'$-part of $x$. Then $hat(lambda)$ is a virtual
  $K$-character of $G$.
] <th:brauer-lift-virtual-character>
#idx("Brauer lift", target: [@th:brauer-lift-virtual-character])
#proof[
  It is clear that $hat(lambda) in cf_K lr((G))$. By Brauer's criterion for
  virtual characters (@th:brauer-virtual-character-criterion), it is sufficient
  to prove that $hat(lambda)_H in "ch" K H$ for every elementary subgroup $H$ of
  $G$. Such a subgroup can be expressed as a direct product
  #source(458)
  $H=A times B$, where $A$ is a $p'$-group and $B$ is a $p$-group. The
  restriction $L_H$ is a $k H$-module whose Brauer character is $lambda_H$, and
  $hat(lambda)_H$ has the same relation to $lambda_H$ as in the general case.
  Thus it is sufficient to prove the theorem for an elementary group
  $H=A times B$, and a $k H$-module $L$ with Brauer character $lambda$, and
  where $hat(lambda)$ is defined as in the statement of the theorem. Now
  $lambda_A$ is the Brauer character of the $k A$-module $L_A$. Since $A$ is a
  $p'$-group, we can apply Corollary~@cor:prime-to-p-brauer-ordinary-characters
  to find a $K A$-module $M$ whose $K$-character coincides with $lambda_A$. Then
  the outer tensor product $M #sym.hash 1_B$ of $M$ with the trivial
  $K B$-module $1_B$ is a $K H$-module whose character clearly coincides with
  $hat(lambda)$, and the proof is finished.
]

#definition[
  The virtual $K$-character $hat(lambda)$, associated with a Brauer character
  $lambda$ of $G$ as in @th:brauer-lift-virtual-character, is called the _Brauer
  lift_ of $lambda$.
] <def:brauer-lift>
#idx("Brauer lift", target: [@def:brauer-lift])

The Brauer lift was first defined by Green @bib:Green1955b, and played a
fundamental role in his determination of the irreducible complex-valued
characters of the general linear groups over finite fields.

#corollary[
  The decomposition map
  $ d:G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((k G)) $
  is surjective.
] <cor:decomposition-map-surjective>
#proof[
  By @prop:decomposition-brauer-character-square, it is sufficient to prove that
  each Brauer character $lambda$ is the restriction $hat(lambda)_(G_(p'))$ of
  its Brauer lift $hat(lambda)$, and this is clear from the definition.
]

#corollary[
  The map $e:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((K G))$ is injective.
] <cor:projective-character-map-injective>
#proof[
  Let $u in ker e$. By @th:decomposition-projective-character-adjointness, it
  follows that $i_k lr((u,d z))=0$ for all $z in G_0 lr((K G))$, and so
  $i_k lr((u,v))=0$ for all $v in G_0 lr((k G))$ because $d$ is surjective. By
  @prop:projective-simple-grothendieck-pairing, $K_0 lr((k G))$ and
  $G_0 lr((k G))$ admit dual bases with respect to the form $i_k$; hence $u=0$,
  which proves that $e$ is injective.
]

An immediate consequence of @cor:projective-character-map-injective is the
following interesting result:

#corollary[
  Suppose $P$ and $P' in cal(P)(R G)$ are such that the $K G$-modules
  $K times.o_R P$ and $K times.o_R P'$ are isomorphic. Then $P tilde.equiv P'$
  as $R G$-modules.
] <cor:projective-lattices-determined-by-character>

This result can also be obtained as a special case of Hattori's
Theorem~@th:hattori-projective-lattice-cancellation. A second proof of
@cor:projective-lattices-determined-by-character, using the invertibility of the
Cartan matrix, is given in
Exercise~@exc:projective-character-isomorphism-cartan; see also
@cor:complete-projective-lattices-character-isomorphism.

A final corollary provides some number-theoretic information about the
decomposition matrix.

#source(459)
#corollary[
  The invariant factors of the $s times r$ matrix $bold(D)$ are ${1,1,dots,1}$
  ($r$ times). For $1<=h<=r$, the G.C.D. of the $h$-rowed minors of $bold(D)$ is
  equal to $1$. The matrix $overline(bold(D))$ has rank $r$ over the field $k$.
] <cor:decomposition-matrix-primitive-columns>
#proof[
  The decomposition matrix $bold(D)=(d_(i j))$ is the transpose of the matrix of
  the decomposition map $d:G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ with respect to the
  bases ${[Z_i]}_(1<=i<=s)$ and ${[F_j]}_(1<=j<=r)$. Thus
  $ d[Z_i]=sum_(j=1)^r d_(i j)[F_j],1<=i<=s. $
  By the Invariant Factor Theorem (@bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR (16.8)]) applied to
  the pair of $ZZ$-modules $d(G_0 lr((K G))) subset.eq G_0 lr((k G))$, we can
  find a $ZZ$-basis ${f_1,dots,f_r}$ of $G_0 lr((k G))$, and positive integers
  $delta_1,dots,delta_t$ for some $t<=r$, such that
  ${delta_1 f_1,dots,delta_t f_t}$ is a $ZZ$-basis for $d(G_0 lr((K G)))$, and
  $delta_i divides delta_(i+1)$ for each $i$. These ${delta_i}$ are the
  _invariant factors_ of $bold(D)$. From @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR §16], this
  corresponds to finding $ZZ$-unimodular matrices $bold(X)^(s times s)$ and
  $bold(Y)^(r times r)$ such that
  $
    bold(X)bold(D)bold(Y)=mat(
      delim: "[",
      delta_1, , , , ;
      , dots.down, , , ;
      , , delta_t, , ;
      , , , 0, ;
      , , , , dots.down;
      , , , , , 0
    ).
  $
  Since $d$ is surjective by @cor:decomposition-map-surjective, we deduce that
  $t=r$, and each of the invariant factors ${delta_i}_(1<=i<=r)$ is equal to
  $1$. From @bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR §16], this implies that the G.C.D. of the
  $h$-rowed minors of $bold(D)$ is equal to $1$, for $1<=h<=r$. Finally, since
  $overline(bold(X))$ and $overline(bold(Y))$ are invertible matrices over $k$,
  and
  $
    overline(bold(X))overline(bold(D))overline(bold(Y))=
    mat(delim: "[", 1, , ; , dots.down, ; , , 1),
  $
  it follows that $overline(bold(D))$ has rank $r$, and the proof is completed.
]

#heading(level: 3)[
  Orthogonality Relations for Brauer Characters
] <ss:brauer-character-orthogonality>

We keep the notation and assumptions of §@ss:cartan-brauer-large-field, that is,
$(K,R,k)$ is a $p$-modular system with $char K=0$, such that $K$ is sufficiently
large relative to $G$, and $R G$ is a semiperfect ring.

We first extend the character table data (@def:character-table-data) to
encompass Brauer characters. Recall from @cor:brauer-number-simple-modules that
the number of simple $k G$-modules is equal to the number $r$ of $p$-regular
classes in $G$. See also @eq:brauer-character-table-data.

#source(460)
#idx("character", "table")
#statement("Brauer Character Table Data", "def")[
  ${Z_i}$, ${zeta^i}$, $z_i=deg zeta^i$, ${frak(C)_i}$, $h_i=|frak(C)_i|$,
  $zeta_j^i$, $zeta_(j ast)^i$ as in @def:character-table-data, for $1<=i,j<=s$.
  (We write $zeta^i$ instead of $zeta^((i))$, for convenience.)

  $
    {frak(C)_1={1},frak(C)_2,dots,frak(C)_r}
    =p"-regular conjugacy classes in" G.
  $
  $ {F_1=1_G,dots,F_r}="basic set of simple" k G"-modules." $
  $ {phi^1,dots,phi^r}="Brauer characters afforded by" F_1,dots,F_r. $
  ${U_1,dots,U_r}=$ indecomposable projective modules, with $U_i$ the projective
  cover of $F_i$, $1<=i<=r$. The ${U_i}$ are also called _principal
  indecomposable modules_.
  $ {eta^1,dots,eta^r}="Brauer characters of" {U_1,dots,U_r}. $
  ${P_1,dots,P_r}=$ basic set of indecomposable projective $R G$-modules, with
  $overline(P_i)=U_i$, $1<=i<=r$.

  $phi_j^i$, $phi_(j ast)^i$, $eta_j^i$, $eta_(j ast)^i$, $1<=i,j<=r$, values of
  the Brauer characters $phi^i$, $eta^i$ on the classes $frak(C)_j$,
  $frak(C)_(j ast)$, respectively.

  $ bold(C)=(c_(i j))="Cartan matrix." $
  $ bold(D)=(d_(i j))="decomposition matrix." $
  $
    bold(Phi)=(phi_j^i)^(r times r),bold(H)=(eta_j^i)^(r times r),
    bold(Z)=(zeta_j^i)^(s times r).
  $

  By @prop:decomposition-brauer-character-square and
  @cor:brauer-character-composition-factors, we have
  $ zeta^i|_(G_(p'))=sum_(j=1)^r d_(i j)phi^j,1<=i<=s, $
  <eq:ordinary-brauer-decomposition>
  $ eta^i=sum_(j=1)^r c_(i j)phi^j,1<=i<=r. $
  <eq:projective-brauer-cartan-decomposition>
  From these results, we obtain,
  $ bold(Z)=bold(D)bold(Phi),quad bold(H)=bold(C)bold(Phi) $
  <eq:brauer-character-matrix-identities>
] <def:brauer-character-table-data>

#lemma[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$bold(Phi)$ is unimodular over $R$.],
    [$det bold(C)!=0$.],
  )
] <lem:brauer-table-unit-cartan-nonsingular>
#proof[
  By @cor:modular-system-sufficiently-large-fields, $k$ is a splitting field for
  $G$, so by @th:modular-irreducible-character-independence the $k$-characters
  of ${F_1,dots,F_r}$ are linearly independent over $k$. These characters are
  determined by the rows of the matrix $overline(bold(Phi))$, by
  @prop:brauer-character-properties(ii) and thus $det overline(bold(Phi))!=0$ in
  $k$. Since $R$ is a local ring with residue field $k$, $det bold(Phi)$ is a
  unit in $R$, proving (i).

  #source(461)
  In order to prove (ii), we first apply the Second Orthogonality Relation
  @prop:first-second-character-orthogonality to obtain
  $
    sum_(m=1)^s zeta_i^m zeta_j^m
    =(|G|h_i^(-1)delta_(i,j ast))^(r times r)
  $
  and hence $det attach(bold(Z), tl: t)bold(Z)!=0$. By
  @eq:brauer-character-matrix-identities and
  @cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix we have
  $
    attach(bold(Z), tl: t)bold(Z)
    =attach(bold(Phi), tl: t)attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(D)bold(Phi)
    =attach(bold(Phi), tl: t)bold(C)bold(Phi).
  $
  Since $det attach(bold(Z), tl: t)bold(Z)!=0$ and $bold(Phi)$ is unimodular by
  part (i), we obtain $det bold(C)!=0$, and the lemma is proved.
]

#theorem(title: [Orthogonality relations for Brauer characters])[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$sum_(j=1)^r h_j phi_j^i eta_(j ast)^k=|G|delta_(i k)$.],
    [$sum_(j=1)^r h_j phi_j^i phi_(j ast)^k=|G|gamma_(i k)$, where
      $bold(C)^(-1)=(gamma_(i j))$.],
    [$sum_(j=1)^r h_j eta_j^i eta_(j ast)^k=|G|c_(i k)$.],
  )
] <th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations>
#idx(
  "orthogonality relations",
  target: [@th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations],
)
#proof[
  We first note that $bold(C)$ is invertible by
  Lemma~@lem:brauer-table-unit-cartan-nonsingular, so that $bold(C)^(-1)$ is
  defined. From the proof of @lem:brauer-table-unit-cartan-nonsingular,
  @eq:brauer-character-matrix-identities and
  @cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix, we have:
  $
    attach(bold(Z), tl: t)bold(Z)=bold(Y),quad bold(Z)=bold(D)bold(Phi),
    quad bold(H)=bold(C)bold(Phi)=attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(Z),
  $
  where
  $ bold(Y)=(|G|h_i^(-1)delta_(i,j ast)). $
  From these we obtain
  $
    bold(Phi)bold(Y)^(-1)attach(bold(Phi), tl: t)=bold(C)^(-1),
    quad bold(Phi)bold(Y)^(-1)attach(bold(H), tl: t)=1,
    quad bold(H)bold(Y)^(-1)attach(bold(H), tl: t)=bold(C).
  $
  These formulas in turn imply (ii), (i), and (iii) above, completing the proof.
]

Before obtaining some interesting consequences, we need a variation on the
Brauer lift (@th:brauer-lift-virtual-character), as follows:

#lemma[
  Let $|G|=p^a m$, with $p divides.not m$. Let $lambda$ be a Brauer character of
  a $k G$-module $L$. Define
  $
    theta(x)=cases(
      p^a lambda(x) & quad x in G_(p'),
      0 & quad x in.not G_(p').
    )
  $
  Then $theta$ is a virtual character of $G$.
] <lem:p-part-supported-brauer-lift>
#proof[
  #source(462)
  By Brauer's criterion (@th:brauer-virtual-character-criterion), we have to
  prove that $theta|_H$ is a virtual character of $H$ for every elementary
  subgroup $H$. As in the proof of @th:brauer-lift-virtual-character, we can
  express $H$ in the form $H=A times B$, for a $p'$-subgroup $A$ and a $p$-group
  $B$ of order $p^t$, where $t<=a$. Let $rho_B$ be the regular character of $B$,
  and define a $K$-valued function $psi$ on $H$ by
  $ psi(a b)=lambda(a)rho_B lr((b)),a in A,b in B. $
  From the proof of Theorem~@th:brauer-lift-virtual-character, $lambda|_A$ is a
  $K$-character of $A$, and hence $psi$ is a $K$-character of $H$. For
  $x in H_(p')$, we have $x in A$, and
  $ psi(x)=|B|lambda(x)=p^t lambda(x)=p^(t-a)theta(x). $
  Then $theta(x)=p^(a-t)psi(x)$. The same formula holds for $p$-irregular $x$ in
  $H$, since $rho_B$ vanishes on the elements in $B-{1}$. Thus
  $theta in "ch" K H$ for every elementary subgroup $H$, so $theta in "ch" K G$,
  as required.
]

#theorem[
  $det bold(C)=p^l$, for some nonnegative integer $l$.
] <th:cartan-determinant-p-power>
#proof[
  Let $|G|=p^a m$ as in @lem:p-part-supported-brauer-lift, and let
  $theta_i in "ch" K G$ be the virtual character defined in
  @lem:p-part-supported-brauer-lift corresponding to the irreducible Brauer
  character $phi^i$, for $1<=i<=r$. Let $theta_i=sum_(j=1)^s a_(i j)zeta^j$,
  where $a_(i j) in ZZ$, for $1<=i<=r$. Then from
  @prop:character-scalar-products-multiplicities, we have
  $
    a_(i j) & =(theta_i,zeta^j)
              =|G|^(-1)sum_(x in G) theta_i lr((x^(-1)))zeta^j lr((x)) \
            & =p^a |G|^(-1)sum_(x in G_(p')) phi^i lr((x^(-1)))zeta^j lr((x)) \
            & =p^a |G|^(-1)sum_(x in G_(p')) phi^i lr((x^(-1)))
              sum_(k=1)^r d_(j k)phi^k lr((x)) \
            & =p^a sum_(k=1)^r d_(j k)
              { |G|^(-1)sum_(x in G_(p')) phi^i lr((x^(-1)))phi^k lr((x)) }
              =p^a sum_(k=1)^r d_(j k)gamma_(k i),
  $
  where $bold(C)^(-1)=(gamma_(i j))$, by
  @th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations(ii). But $bold(C)^(-1)$ is a
  symmetric matrix, and $bold(A)=(a_(i j))$ satisfies the equation
  $ bold(A)=p^a bold(C)^(-1)attach(bold(D), tl: t). $
  Then, by @cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix,
  $
    bold(A) dot attach(bold(A), tl: t)
    =p^(2a)bold(C)^(-1)attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(D)attach(bold(C)^(-1), tl: t)
    =p^(2a)bold(C)^(-1).
  $
  Thus
  $ (det bold(C))(det(bold(A) dot attach(bold(A), tl: t)))="power of" p, $
  completing the proof. For a more conceptual proof, see
  @th:cartan-map-cokernel-p-primary and @cor:cartan-determinant-arbitrary-field
  below.
]

#source(463)
#theorem[
  Let ${P_1,dots,P_r}$ be a basic set of indecomposable projective
  $R G$-modules, as in @def:brauer-character-table-data, and let $tau_i$ be the
  $K$-character afforded by the $K G$-module $K times.o_R P_i$, $1<=i<=r$. Then
  the following statements hold:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [$tau_i=sum_(j=1)^s d_(j i)zeta^j,1<=i<=r$.],
    [$tau_i lr((x))=0$ for all $p$-irregular $x in G$.],
    [${tau_1,dots,tau_r}$ form a $ZZ$-basis for the set of virtual
      $K$-characters of $G$ which vanish on the $p$-irregular elements of $G$.],
  )
] <th:projective-characters-supported-p-regular>
#proof[
  Fix $i$ between $1$ and $r$. By
  Proposition~@prop:brauer-character-properties(iv), $tau_i|_(G_(p'))=eta^i$
  since $overline(P_i)=U_i$. By
  @th:decomposition-projective-character-adjointness and
  @prop:projective-simple-grothendieck-pairing, we have
  $
    i_K lr(([K times.o_R P_i],[Z_j]))
    & =i_k lr(([U_i],[overline(Z_j)])) \
    & =i_k lr(([U_i],sum_(h=1)^r d_(j h)[F_h]))=d_(j i).
  $
  Since $tau_i$ is the character afforded by $K times.o_R P_i$, we have
  $(tau_i,zeta^j)=d_(j i)$ by the definition of the bilinear form $i_K$; hence
  $ tau_i=sum_(j=1)^s d_(j i)zeta^j, $
  proving (i). This also shows that the ${tau_i}$ are linearly independent, by
  @cor:decomposition-matrix-primitive-columns.

  We next prove that each character $tau_i$ vanishes on the $p$-irregular
  classes of $G$. From the orthogonality relations for the ${zeta^j}$ and
  @cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix, we obtain
  $
    |G|^(-1)sum_(x in G)|tau_i lr((x))|^2
    =sum_(j=1)^s d_(j i)^2=c_(i i).
  $
  On the other hand, from @th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations we have
  $
    |G|^(-1)sum_(y in G_(p'))|tau_i lr((y))|^2
    =|G|^(-1)sum_(j=1)^r h_j eta_j^i eta_(j ast)^i=c_(i i).
  $
  Comparing these results, we see that $tau_i lr((x))=0$ for all
  $x in.not G_(p')$, and (ii) is established.

  Finally, to prove (iii), we have to show that if $theta in "ch" K G$ is a
  virtual character vanishing on the $p$-irregular classes, then $theta$ is a
  $ZZ$-linear combination of the ${tau_i}$. We put
  $ theta=sum a_i tau_i, $
  #source(464)
  and try to solve for coefficients ${a_i}$ in $ZZ$. The problem is to solve the
  equations
  $
    theta(x_i)=sum_(j=1)^r a_j tau_j lr((x_i))
    =sum_(j=1)^r a_j eta_i^j,quad "where" x_i in frak(C)_i,1<=i<=r.
  $
  The coefficient matrix is $bold(H)=bold(C)bold(Phi)$, and is invertible since
  both $bold(C)$ and $bold(Phi)$ are invertible. It remains to show that the
  solutions ${a_i}$ are integers. By
  Theorem~@th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations, we have
  $ a_j=|G|^(-1)sum_(i=1)^r h_i theta(x_i)phi_(i ast)^j. $
  Since the decomposition map is surjective by
  @cor:decomposition-map-surjective, we may write
  $ phi_(i ast)^j=sum_(k=1)^s m_(j k)zeta_(i ast)^k $
  for some integers ${m_(j k)}$. Since $theta$ is a virtual character vanishing
  on the $p$-irregular classes, we obtain
  $
    a_j=|G|^(-1)sum_(i=1)^r sum_(k=1)^s
    h_i theta(x_i)m_(j k)zeta_(i ast)^k
    =sum_(k=1)^s m_(j k)(theta,zeta^k) in ZZ,
  $
  completing the proof. For another proof of (ii), see
  Theorem~@th:projective-character-singular-vanishing.

]

We can now state the following additional properties of the Cartan-Brauer
Triangle (@prop:cartan-brauer-commutative-triangle):

#proposition[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The Cartan homomorphism $c:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ is injective,
      and $op("cok") c$ ($=G_0 lr((k G)) slash im c$) is a finite group of order
      a power of $p$.],
    [$im e$ consists of all $alpha in G_0 lr((K G))$ whose virtual character
      $alpha$ (see @prop:grothendieck-character-ring) vanishes on the
      $p$-irregular classes.],
    [The map $e:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((K G))$ is a split injection, that is,
      $e$ is injective and $im e$ is a $ZZ$-direct summand of $G_0 lr((K G))$.],
  )
] <prop:cartan-triangle-injectivity-cokernel>
#proof[
  Assertion (i) is a restatement of @th:cartan-determinant-p-power, while (ii)
  is a direct consequence of the definition of $e$ and
  @th:projective-characters-supported-p-regular(iii). Further, $e$ is injective
  by @cor:projective-character-map-injective. Finally, by assertion (ii) we can
  identify $im e$ with the $ZZ$-submodule of $"ch" K G$ consisting of all
  virtual characters which vanish outside $G_(p')$. Therefore $im e$ is a
  $ZZ$-pure submodule of $"ch" K G$, and hence (see
  §@ss:modules-dedekind-domains) is a direct summand of $"ch" K G$. This
  completes the proof.
]

#source(465)
Another proof of @prop:cartan-triangle-injectivity-cokernel(iii) is given in
Steps 1 and 2 of the proof of @th:projective-lattice-character-image below. See
also @th:projective-character-map-split-injection.

We conclude this section with a number of interesting examples, and begin with:

(i) _Characters of defect zero._ The _$p$-defect_ (or simply _defect_) of
$zeta in Irr G$ is the exponent to which $p$ divides $|G| slash zeta(1)$. Thus
$zeta$ is of _defect zero_ if $|G|_p$ divides $zeta(1)$ (see
@bib:CurtisReiner1962[CR §86]).#idx("defect")

#proposition[
  Let $zeta in Irr G$ be a character of defect zero. Then $zeta=tau_i$ for some
  character $tau_i$, $1<=i<=r$, afforded by $K P_i$, where $P_i$ is a projective
  indecomposable $R G$-module. Moreover, $overline(P_i)$ is a simple
  $k G$-module, and is also a projective $k G$-module.
] <prop:defect-zero-character-projective-simple>
#proof[
  Let $Z$ be a simple $K G$-module affording $zeta$. Since $zeta$ has defect
  zero, the central primitive idempotent $epsilon$ corresponding to $zeta$
  belongs to $R G$, by @prop:group-character-orthogonality-idempotents(ii). For
  some indecomposable projective $R G$-module $P_i$, $Z$ is a composition factor
  of $K P_i$ ($=K times.o_R P_i$). Then $epsilon P_i!=0$. If $epsilon P_i!=P_i$,
  then since $epsilon$ is a central idempotent of $R G$, the formula
  $P_i=epsilon P_i plus.o (1-epsilon)P_i$ gives an $R G$-decomposition of $P_i$,
  which is impossible. Therefore $epsilon P_i=P_i$, which implies at once that
  $tau_i=a zeta$ for some positive integer $a$.

  It now follows from @th:projective-characters-supported-p-regular(ii) that
  $zeta$ vanishes on all $p$-irregular elements of $G$. Hence by
  @th:projective-characters-supported-p-regular(iii), $zeta$ is expressible as a
  $ZZ$-linear combination of the ${tau_j}_(1<=j<=r)$. Since $zeta=a^(-1)tau_i$,
  this proves that $a=1$, and that furthermore the index $i$ is uniquely
  determined by $zeta$.

  We deduce that exactly one of the decomposition numbers ${d_(j i)}$ in
  @th:projective-characters-supported-p-regular(i) is nonzero, and must equal
  $1$. Since $bold(C)=attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(D)$, this implies that
  $overline(P_i)$ is simple. Thus we have $overline(P_i)=U_i=F_i$, completing
  the proof. (Gallagher's Theorem~@th:gallagher-character-vanishing is an easy
  consequence of this Theorem.)
]

Our next example shows how characters of defect zero may arise:

(ii) _The Steinberg character of $op("SL")_2 lr((q))$._
#idx("Steinberg character")
Let $G=op("SL")_2 lr((q))$, the group of $2 times 2$ matrices of determinant $1$
over a finite field $FF_q$ of $q$ elements, and let $p$ be the characteristic of
$FF_q$. Let $B$ be the subgroup of $G$ consisting of all upper triangular
matrices. As an exercise, we ask the reader to show that
$ G=B union (B x B), $
for some $x in.not B$. From Exercise~@exc:doubly-transitive-character it follows
that the permutation character $theta=(1_B)^G$ satisfies $(theta,theta)=2$, and
hence $theta=1_G+zeta$ for an irreducible character $zeta$ of degree $|G:B|-1$.
Since $|G|=q(q^2-1)$ and $|B|=q(q-1)$, we have $|G:B|=q+1$, and $zeta(1)=q$.
Then $zeta$ is an irreducible character of $p$-defect zero, and satisfies all
the conditions of Proposition~@prop:defect-zero-character-projective-simple. The
character $zeta$ is called the
#source(466)
_Steinberg character_ of $G$. In Chapter~7 of Volume~II we shall prove that all
finite groups with $B N$-pairs have a Steinberg character which behaves
according to @prop:defect-zero-character-projective-simple, and plays a special
role in the representation theory of $G$. (See Steinberg @bib:Steinberg1956,
@bib:Steinberg1957 for the construction of a representation affording the
Steinberg characters of Chevalley groups, and Curtis @bib:Curtis1966 for a
construction of the character for finite groups with a $B N$-pair.)

As a final example, we continue the discussion of Example (ii) following
@def:decomposition-map-matrix. Let $G=S_4$, $p=2$, and let $(QQ,R,k)$ be the
$2$-modular system in which $R$ is the ring of $2$-adic integers in the rational
field $QQ$, and $k=R slash 2R tilde.equiv ZZ slash 2ZZ$. The character table of
$S_4$ is given at the end of §@ss:character-table. The reader should verify that
the Brauer character table is given by
#table(
  columns: 3,
  stroke: none,
  table.hline(y: 1),
  table.vline(x: 1),
  table.header([], [$x_1$], [$x_3$]),
  [$phi^1$], [1], [1],
  [$phi^2$], [2], [$-1$],
)
where $x_1=1$, $x_3=(123)$. The formula $bold(C)=attach(bold(D), tl: t)bold(D)$
gives
$ bold(C)=mat(4, 2; 2, 3). $
Further, the characters of $G$ afforded by the indecomposable projective
$R G$-modules are
$
  tau_1=sum_j d_(j 1)zeta^j=zeta^1+zeta^2+zeta^4+zeta^5 \
  tau_2=sum_j d_(j 2)zeta^j=zeta^3+zeta^4+zeta^5,
$
and both of these vanish on the $2$-irregular classes. There are two
indecomposable projective $k G$-modules $U_1,U_2$, both of $k$-dimension $8$.

The reader should now carry out a similar calculation for the case where $p=3$.
In this situation, the characters $zeta^4$ and $zeta^5$ have defect zero.

#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Using the notation of @prop:projective-simple-grothendieck-pairing, show that
  $ dim_k Hom_(k G)(U_i,U_j)=c_(i j),1<=i,j<=r. $
  #hint([@exc:cartan-projective-hom-dimensions])[
    The left hand expression equals $i_k lr(([U_i],[U_j]))$, and
    $[U_j]=sum_m c_(j m)[F_m]$ in $G_0 lr((k G))$.
  ]
] <exc:cartan-projective-hom-dimensions>

#exercise[
  Let $G$ be a $p$-group, $k$ a field of characteristic $p$. Show that $k G$ has
  a unique minimal left ideal, given by $k dot s$, where
  $ s=sum_(sigma in G) sigma. $
] <exc:p-group-algebra-socle-sum>

#source(467)
#exercise[
  Let $k$ be a field of characteristic $p$, $E$ a finite Galois extension of $k$
  whose Galois group $G$ is a $p$-group. Let $T:E -> k$ be the trace map, and
  let $alpha in E$ be such that $T(alpha)!=0$. Prove that $alpha$ generates a
  normal basis for $E$ over $k$, that is,
  $ E=plus.o.big_(sigma in G)k dot sigma(alpha). $
  Show further that such elements $alpha$ exist.
  #hint([@exc:p-extension-normal-basis-trace])[
    (Childs-Orzech @bib:Childs1981). It suffices to show that the elements
    ${sigma(alpha):sigma in G}$ are linearly independent over $k$. Suppose not,
    so $xi_0 alpha=0$ for some nonzero $xi_0 in k G$. Then
    $ {xi in k G:xi alpha=0} $
    is a nonzero ideal of $k G$, and therefore contains $sum_(sigma in G)sigma$
    by the preceding Exercise. Thus $(sum_sigma sigma)alpha=0$, that is,
    $T(alpha)=0$.

    To prove the existence of elements of $E$ of nonzero trace, use the fact
    that $E$ is separable over $k$; see §@ss:separable-algebras-modules and
    Exercise~@exc:separable-field-nonzero-trace.
  ]
] <exc:p-extension-normal-basis-trace>

#exercise[
  Let $(K,R,k)$ be a $p$-modular system such that $R G$ is a semiperfect ring,
  where $G$ is a finite $p'$-group. Show that
  #enum(
    numbering: "(a)",
    [The simple left $k G$-modules ${F_i:1<=i<=r}$ coincide with the projective
      indecomposable modules ${U_i:1<=i<=r}$.],
    [For each $i$, $1<=i<=r$, there exists a projective $R G$-lattice $P_i$ such
      that $overline(P_i)=F_i$, where bar denotes reduction mod $frak(p)$.],
    [For each $i$, $K P_i$ ($=K times.o_R P_i$) is a simple left $K G$-module
      such that $d[K P_i]=[F_i]$, where $d$ is the decomposition map defined in
      @prop:decomposition-homomorphism-existence.],
    [The modules ${K P_i:1<=i<=r}$ are a basic set of simple left
      $K G$-modules.],
  )
  #hint([@exc:prime-to-p-projective-lifts])[
    Both $K G$ and $k G$ are semisimple, since $|G|!=0$ in both $K$ and $k$.
    Then $R G slash rad R G tilde.equiv k G$, $rad k G=0$, so (a) and (b)
    follows from @prop:projective-cover-summary. By definition of $d$, we have
    $d[K P_i]=F_i$, and $K P_i$ is therefore simple. Finally, $K G$ is a direct
    sum of copies of the ${K P_i}$, since $R G$ is a direct sum of copies of the
    lattices ${P_i}$; this implies that (d) is valid.

    Another proof of these assertions is given in
    @cor:prime-to-p-brauer-ordinary-characters, using the formula for the Cartan
    matrix in terms of the decomposition matrix. A related result is given in
    @th:invertible-group-order-lattice-isomorphism below; see also
    Exercise~@exc:invertible-group-order-projective-lattice.

    As pointed out at the beginning of §@ss:cartan-map-triangle, the ring $R G$
    will be semiperfect whenever $K$ is sufficiently large relative to $G$, and
    also whenever $R$ is a complete d.v.r. Without such a restriction on $R G$,
    assertion (b) above need not be valid, since idempotents in $k G$ need not
    come from idempotents in $R G$. For example, let $R$ be the $2$-adic
    valuation ring in the rational field $QQ$, and let $G$ be a cyclic group of
    order $7$. Then $k=ZZ slash 2ZZ$, and $G$ has $3$ irreducible
    representations over $k$, since $x^7-1=(x-1)f(x)g(x)$ in $k[x]$, with
    $f(x)$, $g(x)$ distinct irreducible polynomials of degree $3$. On the other
    hand, $G$ has only $2$ irreducible representations over $QQ$, since
    $x^7-1=(x-1)Phi_7 lr((x))$ in $QQ[x]$, where $Phi_7 lr((x))$ is the
    cyclotomic polynomial of order $7$ and degree $6$.
  ]
] <exc:prime-to-p-projective-lifts>

#source(468)
In the remaining exercises, $G$ denotes a finite group, and $(K,R,k)$ a
$p$-modular system such that $K$ is sufficiently large relative to $G$.

#exercise[
  For each $i$, $1<=i<=r$, let $eta^i$ denote the Brauer character of the
  indecomposable projective $k G$-module $U_i$. Prove that the degree
  $eta^i lr((1))$ is a multiple of $|G|_p$.
  #hint([@exc:projective-degree-p-part-divisibility])[
    Let $S$ be a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$. Then $k G$ is a free left
    $k S$-module. Deduce from this that the restriction $(U_i)_S$ is a
    projective left $k S$-module, and hence a free left $k S$-module, since
    $k S$ is a local ring (see @th:p-group-algebra-local). Also see Example
    @exm:indecomposable-projective-group-lattices[(i)] following
    @th:vertices-going-up.
  ]
] <exc:projective-degree-p-part-divisibility>

#exercise[
  Prove that the diagonal entry $c_(i i)$ of the Cartan matrix is $>=2$ unless
  the corresponding module $U_i$ is a simple $k G$-module.
  #hint([@exc:cartan-diagonal-simple-projective])[
    Apply @prop:projective-cover-summary(vi).
  ]
] <exc:cartan-diagonal-simple-projective>

#exercise[
  Let $G$ be an elementary group (see Definition~@def:elementary-subgroup).
  Prove that the image of the Cartan map $c:K_0 lr((k G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ is
  $|G|_p dot G_0 lr((k G))$.
  #hint([@exc:elementary-group-cartan-image])[
    Write $G$ in the form $H times S$, where $H$ is a $p'$-group and $S$ is a
    $p$-group, so $|S|=|G|_p$. Then $k H=plus.o.big_i V_i$, where the ${V_i}$
    are simple left $k H$-modules, not necessarily distinct. Since
    $k G tilde.equiv k H times.o_k k S$ as $k$-algebras, it follows that
    $ k G tilde.equiv plus.o.big_i (V_i #sym.hash k S), $
    where $V_i #sym.hash k S$ is an outer tensor product. This gives the
    decomposition of $k G$ into indecomposable projective summands. Since the
    trivial $S$-module $k$ occurs with multiplicity $|S|$ as composition factor
    of $k S$, it follows that
    $ c[V_i #sym.hash k S]=|S|[V_i] quad "in" G_0 lr((k G)). $

    Another approach is as follows: By Exercise~@exc:direct-product-characters,
    $ Irr_K G={lambda mu:lambda in Irr_K H,mu in Irr_K S}. $
    Extend each $lambda$ to a character $lambda^*$ on $G$ which is trivial on
    $S$. By @cor:brauer-number-simple-modules and
    @cor:prime-to-p-brauer-ordinary-characters, the $lambda$'s are the
    irreducible Brauer characters of $H$, and the $lambda^*$'s those of $G$. Now
    compute the decomposition matrix of $d:G_0 lr((K G)) -> G_0 lr((k G))$ from
    the formula
    $ (lambda mu)|_(G_(p'))=mu(1)lambda^*, $
    and then use @cor:cartan-decomposition-gram-matrix to find the Cartan matrix
    of $G$.
  ]
] <exc:elementary-group-cartan-image>

#exercise[
  (Gustafson). Prove that every simple $k G$-module is one-dimensional if and
  only if $G=S ⋊ A$, where $A$ is abelian and $S$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of
  $G$.
] <exc:one-dimensional-modular-simples>

#exercise[
  For $H<=G$, prove that there is a commutative diagram of additive groups and
  homomorphisms:
  #induction-cartan-square
  #source(469)
  Here, the $d$'s and $c$'s are decomposition maps and Cartan maps,
  respectively, and maps $"ind"_H^G$ are given by
  $ "ind"_H^G lr([M])=[M^G], $
  where $M$ is either a $K H$- or a $k H$-module.
  #hint([@exc:induction-cartan-commutative-diagram])[
    See §@ss:induction-positive-characteristic if need be.
  ]
] <exc:induction-cartan-commutative-diagram>
#idx("induction", target: [@exc:induction-cartan-commutative-diagram])

#exercise[
  Let $H<=G$, and let $L$ be a $k H$-module with Brauer character $lambda$.
  Define a class function $lambda^G$ on $G_(p')$ by the formula
  $
    lambda^G lr((x))=|H|^(-1)sum_(y in G) dot(lambda) lr((y x y^(-1))),
    x in G_(p'),
  $
  as in @eq:induced-character-average. Prove that $lambda^G$ is the Brauer
  character of the induced $k G$-module $L^G$.
  #hint([@exc:induced-brauer-character])[
    By @th:brauer-lift-virtual-character, there exists a virtual character
    $hat(lambda)$ of $H$ such that $hat(lambda)|_(H_(p'))=lambda$. Then
    $hat(lambda)=mu-nu$, where $mu$ and $nu$ are $K$-characters of $H$ afforded
    by $K H$-modules $M$ and $N$, respectively. We have $d([M]-[N])=[L]$ in
    $G_0 lr((k H))$, by @prop:brauer-character-properties(iv) and
    @prop:decomposition-brauer-character-square. By
    Exercise~@exc:induction-cartan-commutative-diagram, we obtain
    $ d([M^G]-[N^G])=[L^G] quad "in" G_0 lr((k G)). $
    Again using @prop:brauer-character-properties(iv) and
    @prop:decomposition-brauer-character-square, it follows that the Brauer
    character of $L^G$ is $(mu-nu)^G|_(G_(p'))$, and this restriction is
    $lambda^G$. (For a different proof, see @bib:Brauer1941, p. 580.)
  ]
] <exc:induced-brauer-character>
#idx("character", "induced", target: [@exc:induced-brauer-character])
#idx("induced character", target: [@exc:induced-brauer-character])

#exercise[
  Let $H<=G$, and let ${phi^i,eta^i}$ be defined as in
  @def:brauer-character-table-data for $G$, and let the corresponding types of
  Brauer characters for $H$ be given by ${hat(phi)^i,hat(eta)^i}$. Prove the
  formulas of Nakayama (see @bib:Brauer1941, §26), which are as follows: There
  exists non-negative integers ${a_(i j)}$ and ${b_(i j)}$ such that
  $
    (hat(eta)^i)^G=sum_j a_(i j)eta^j quad "on" G_(p'),
    quad phi^j|_(H_(p'))=sum_i a_(i j)hat(phi)^i quad "on" H_(p'), \
    (hat(phi)^i)^G=sum_j b_(i j)phi^j quad "on" G_(p'),
    quad eta^j|_(H_(p'))=sum_i b_(i j)hat(eta)^i quad "on" H_(p').
  $
  #hint([@exc:nakayama-brauer-induction-formulas])[
    For each indecomposable projective $k H$-module $hat(U)_i$, the induced
    module $(hat(U)_i)^G$ is $k G$-projective, so that the desired equation
    holds for $(hat(eta)^i)^G$ for some choice of ${a_(i j)}$. Then
    $
      a_(i j)=|G|^(-1)sum_(x in G_(p')) (hat(eta)^i)^G lr((x))
      phi^j lr((x^(-1)))
    $
    by @th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations(i). The method of proof of
    the Frobenius Reciprocity Formula~@th:frobenius-class-functions then gives
    $
      a_(i j)=|H|^(-1)sum_(y in H_(p')) hat(eta)^i lr((y))
      phi^j lr((y^(-1))),
    $
    which implies the desired formula for $phi^j|_(H_(p'))$, by
    @th:brauer-character-orthogonality-relations(i). The other formulas are
    proved similarly.
  ]
] <exc:nakayama-brauer-induction-formulas>
#idx("Nakayama formulas", target: [@exc:nakayama-brauer-induction-formulas])

#source(470)
#exercise[
  Let $H<=G$, and let $zeta in "Bch" k G$, $xi in "Bch" k H$. Prove that
  $ (zeta|_(H_(p')))xi in "Bch" k H,quad zeta dot xi^G in "Bch" k G, $
  and then show that
  $ zeta dot xi^G={ (zeta|_(H_(p')))xi }^G quad "in" "Bch" k G. $
  #hint([@exc:brauer-character-induction-product])[
    This result is the analogue of @lem:class-function-induction-product for
    Brauer characters. To prove it, use @lem:brauer-character-tensor-product and
    Exercise~@exc:induced-brauer-character, and the method of proof of
    @lem:class-function-induction-product.
  ]
] <exc:brauer-character-induction-product>

#exercise[
  *Brauer Induction Theorem for Brauer Characters.* Prove that
  $ "Bch" k G=sum_(H in cal(E)) ("Bch" k H)^G, $
  where $cal(E)$ is the family of elementary subgroups of $G$, and
  $("Bch" k H)^G$ is the set of all virtual Brauer characters $lambda^G$, for
  $lambda in "Bch" k H$.
  #hint([@exc:brauer-induction-brauer-characters])[
    By the Brauer Induction Theorem~@th:brauer-induction, we have
    $
      1_G=sum a_i lambda_i^G,quad "where" a_i in ZZ,
      lambda_i in "ch" K H_i,H_i in cal(E).
    $
    Then on $G_(p')$ we have $1_G=sum a_i (lambda_i^G)_(G_(p'))$. Using
    @prop:brauer-character-properties(iv), show that for each $i$,
    $
      lambda_i|_((H_i)_(p')) in "Bch" k H_i,quad
      (lambda_i^G)_(G_(p'))="ind"_(H_i)^G lambda_i|_((H_i)_(p')).
    $
    Then use Exercise~@exc:induced-brauer-character to show that the Brauer
    character $1_G$ is a $ZZ$-linear combination of Brauer characters in
    $("Bch" k H_i)^G$, for $H_i in cal(E)$. Finally, use the idea of the proof
    of @th:brauer-induction, together with
    Exercise~@exc:brauer-character-induction-product to complete the proof.
  ]
] <exc:brauer-induction-brauer-characters>
#idx(
  "Brauer Induction Theorem",
  target: [@exc:brauer-induction-brauer-characters],
)
#idx(
  "Induction Theorems",
  "Brauer",
  target: [@exc:brauer-induction-brauer-characters],
)

#exercise[
  Prove @cor:projective-lattices-determined-by-character as follows: Since
  $K times.o_R P tilde.equiv K times.o_R P'$, the $k G$-modules $overline(P)$,
  $overline(P')$ have the same composition factors. Therefore
  $overline(P) tilde.equiv overline(P')$ since $bold(C)$ is non-singular. This
  implies that $P tilde.equiv P'$ by
  @prop:projective-isomorphism-modulo-radical.
] <exc:projective-character-isomorphism-cartan>




